        "content": "<p>I am pretty new to lean and I still tend to rely on basic tactics (e.g., <code>simp</code>, <code>rw</code>, <code>exact</code>) to write my proofs. Recently, I was trying to prove that if <code>H</code> is a Hermitian matrix, then <code>iH</code> is skew adjoint. I recently learned about <code>calc</code>, and my first proof looked like this.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lemma1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">star_def</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">conj_ofReal</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lemma2</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">star_def</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">conj_I</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsHermitian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">skewAdjoint</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">skewAdjoint</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_iff</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">calc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">star_smul</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsHermitian</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isSelfAdjoint</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">star_mul</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">star</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">lemma1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">lemm2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">mul_comm</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_num</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I was wondering if <code>lemma1</code> was already in mathlib, so I tried replacing the proof with <code>hint</code>. I found out that I could close the goal in one line using <code>norm_num</code>. As I understand it, this tactic tries to reduce the lhs and the rhs to a normal form for real/complex numbers.</p>\n<p>After playing around a bit, I found that I could reduce the entire proof to three lines.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsHermitian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">skewAdjoint</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">skewAdjoint</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_iff</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">norm_num</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsHermitian</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isSelfAdjoint</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>As a mathematician, I like the first proof since it makes it clear to me why the theorem is true. As a programmer with an interest in automated verification, I like the second proof since I can dispatch most of the work to a normalization procedure. I was wondering what best practices in Lean say about this. I tried looking through style guide for mathlib, but I couldn't find anything about this. I did notice a preference for <code>simp</code> over <code>simp only [...]</code>. I guess the second proof is more robust to changes in theorem names.</p>\n<p>Sorry if this was a bit long. Thanks for taking the time to read it!</p>",
        "id": 594974778,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Wesley",
        "timestamp": 1778716390
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Welcome! A few points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>hint</code> is somewhat deprecated I believe, <code>try?</code> is the replacement</li>\n<li>As <code>try?</code> suggests, <code>simp</code> solves both of your lemmas (although interestingly they're not in Mathlib AFAICT)</li>\n<li><code>simp [skewAdjoint.mem_iff, h.isSelfAdjoint.star_eq]</code> solves your theorem</li>\n<li>In terms of <code>simp</code> vs <code>simp only</code>, and <code>norm_num</code> or not, one thing to keep in mind is maintainability. Mathlib has a linter called the \"flexible linter\", where <code>norm_num</code> / <code>simp</code> are considered flexible tactics which means their output is somewhat unpredictable (e.g. depends on which theorems are tagged <code>@[simp]</code>), so tactics like <code>rw</code> which fails if the goal doesn't syntactically match what it's trying to rewrite are not allowed to follow flexible tactics. Such proofs break easily. For the same reason we prefer <code>simp</code> only when it's the last tactic (\"terminal simp\").</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 594977470,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1778717281
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And to answer the big \"long and tedious calc proof\" vs \"short and slick norm_num\" question, I think this is relevant:<br>\n<em><a href=\"https://lawrencecpaulson.github.io/2025/10/15/Proofs-trivial.html\">Most proofs are trivial – <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"131965\">Lawrence Paulson</span></a></em><br>\nMost theorems are trivially combining other theorems, and having super short one-line proofs can be a good thing once you get the hang of it. In fact, a long proof can sometimes indicate you can break down an argument into many little theorems each with a one-line proof, and make the proof much more obvious.</p>",
        "id": 594978873,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1778717516
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Small correction: <code>norm_num</code> is (edit: <strong>not</strong>) checked by the flexible linter.</p>",
        "id": 594985770,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1778720185
    }
]